Conduct a Security Control Assessment with OpenSCAP
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure OpenSCAP, SCAP Security Guide, and STIG content on a Linux system
Draft a security assessment plan and assessor independence statement per CGRC Domain 5 practices
Execute automated compliance scans against a STIG baseline
Triage scan findings and document remediation actions
Compile results into a formal Security Assessment Report (SAR)
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line proficiency (package management, file editing, redirecting output)
Familiarity with security compliance concepts (controls, baselines, findings)
Understanding of NIST/DISA STIG terminology (CAT I/II/III severity)
No prior OpenSCAP experience required, but comfort reading XML/HTML reports is helpful
Environment Setup
Al Nafi provides a single Linux machine (RHEL/CentOS/Fedora or Ubuntu) via Start Lab
All work is performed locally — no external cloud services required
Confirm root or sudo access before starting:
sudo whoami
Task 1: Install OpenSCAP, SCAP Security Guide, and STIG Content
RHEL/CentOS/Fedora:

sudo dnf install -y openscap openscap-scanner scap-security-guide
Ubuntu/Debian:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y libopenscap8 ssg-debderived ssg-nondebian
Verify installation:

oscap --version
Locate available STIG/benchmark content:

ls /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/
